capture: workflow engine initialisation and process-definition deployment
runtime: JDK 8
commit: c8f6226105
source: application log during container startup
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no servlet container and no deployment of this commit on the older
  runtime exist on this host, and no six-service reference stack stands behind
  one, so there is no engine-initialisation region to take.  Measured on the
  capture host rather than assumed, and every figure here is an observation.
  TOMCAT_HOME, CATALINA_HOME, CATALINA_BASE, CATALINA_LOG and CATALINA_PID are
  all unset; no container launcher is on PATH and no servlet container is
  installed under any of the usual prefixes; and the hostname arkcase-ce.local
  does not resolve, with the sibling reference-stack and readiness captures
  recording 000 - this tree's token for no HTTP response at all - from every
  documented service URL.  A process engine initialises only inside a running
  application, so with no deployment there is nothing to mine.  One web archive
  does exist in the working tree, and it is deliberately NOT counted as the
  missing deployment satisfied: it is an untracked build output, ignored by the
  repository through the bare pattern at .gitignore:6, and a class taken from
  its own WEB-INF/lib measured class file major version 61, which is the Java 17
  release.  It is therefore a migrated-side artefact and cannot stand in for a
  build of this commit on the older runtime, which is what a baseline requires.
  Two preconditions are deliberately NOT listed among the unmet ones, because
  they are met here and listing them would misstate the reason.  First, the
  older runtime itself is present and answers openjdk version "1.8.0_492".
  Second, a database is reachable on the loopback interface, which matters more
  for this region than for most of its siblings: the engine is wired to a
  datasource and cannot initialise without one, and an unreachable database is
  the very condition under which the engine could not be bootstrapped for
  testing in the first place.  What is missing is a servlet container, a
  deployment of this commit on that runtime, and the reference stack behind it -
  not the JVM and not the database.
risk-classification: residual unverified risk assigned to this gate; not cleared
definitions-expected: 36
definitions-loaded: UNOBSERVED
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full regardless, so that the migrated counterpart reproduces the same
  transformation instead of inventing one.  When this region is captured the
  pipeline is a visible, auditable sed and awk chain that strips timestamps and
  dates, thread names and thread identifiers, session identifiers, generated
  process-instance and deployment identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in
  favour of a stable token, port assignments, and elapsed-time and duration
  figures.  It preserves, above everything else, the count of process
  definitions the engine loaded; and alongside that count it preserves
  process-definition keys and names, deployment names, engine component names,
  lifecycle transitions, and the full message text of every warning and error
  line.  The distinction that matters most in this region, because confusing it
  destroys the only evidence flow 7 has: a GENERATED deployment identifier is
  volatile and must be stripped, while a process-definition KEY is behaviour and
  must be kept.  The two look alike in a log line and are not alike at all.
  Over-normalising here would make the two sides compare equal for a reason
  unrelated to behaviour, which is the failure mode this whole directory exists
  to prevent; a stripped count in particular would erase the region's entire
  purpose.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>,
  which is the convention this documentation tree already uses in its own voice
  at docs/setup.md:78 and applies at docs/setup.md:86, docs/setup.md:87 and
  docs/setup.md:119.  Engine initialisation touches the database, so the
  carriers in scope for this region are specific.  A datasource user and
  password, which an engine that echoes its connection settings would emit, and
  which are recorded only in redacted form; the scan covers password=, passwd,
  user=, secret and token=.  A database connection URL, which is stripped of its
  host, port and database name, because those are environment values rather than
  behaviour and two deployments of identical code against two hosts would
  otherwise compare unequal for no behavioural reason.  And the documented
  administrator credential, which is never transcribed here in any form, not
  even as an illustration of what redaction removes.
