----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.
  Because the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log
  line was invented to fill the gap and no definitions-loaded count was
  invented either.  A fabricated baseline would make every behaviour-preserved
  claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which defeats the
  baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright.  An explicit, machine-readable skip
  with a reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly
  worse than an honest absence.
- Inventing a definitions-loaded count would be the single worst failure
  available in this folder, because flow 7's entire residual-risk assessment
  rests on comparing that one number between the two sides.  The two count
  fields are therefore kept strictly separate and mean different things.
  definitions-expected is a verified repository fact and may always be stated.
  definitions-loaded is an OBSERVATION, so it reads UNOBSERVED here; it is not
  a guess, and it has not been quietly copied from definitions-expected.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record an
  absence in both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches the
  same verdict for the eight application flows; this region is consistent with
  that record rather than an exception to it.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  That script refuses to start without an administrator
  credential supplied through its environment, so no run was possible here even
  setting the absent container and stack aside, and supplying administrator
  material would have been neither available nor appropriate.
- Region-selection rule, stated here so the migrated capture reproduces it
  without guesswork rather than inferring it from this file's shape.  Archive
  the engine-initialisation region of the first-startup window and nothing else:
  the engine configuration being built, schema and table handling, the engine
  reaching a working state, the deployment of the process definitions, and the
  definitions-loaded count.  The region opens when the engine configuration
  begins initialising and closes when the last process definition has been
  deployed, and the whole span is in scope.  Every warning and error line the
  engine emits inside that span belongs to the region as observed.  The
  migrated counterpart must carry the identical filename, the identical field
  set in the identical order, and this identical region selection, so that a
  recursive diff of the two capture directories stays mechanical.
- The filename is workflow-engine-init.log, and it is the name the rest of this
  directory already uses: the sibling spring-context.log allocates this region
  to workflow-engine-init.log by that exact name when it lists its own territory
  boundaries.  The designated producer's current revision writes its
  engine-initialisation region under a different name, process-definitions.log,
  and no file of that name exists in this directory.  The two names denote one
  and the same region, and this deliverable's name is the one the mirror must
  match.  Recording the overlap here prevents a future capture from concluding
  that a second, different region was expected and was missing.
- The engine wiring this region observes, so the capture can be recognised as
  complete rather than merely non-empty.  It is declared at
  acm-tool-integrations/acm-activiti-configuration/src/main/resources/spring/
  spring-library-activiti-configuration.xml.  The engine configuration bean is
  activitiProcessEngineConfiguration, ordered after the schema migration tool it
  declares a depends-on relationship with, bound to the application datasource
  and the shared transaction manager, with schema update enabled and
  transactions declared externally managed.  Schema and table handling runs
  through schemaCommandConfig, produced from commandConfigFactory by the
  transaction-required factory method; the file's own comment at lines 79 to 80
  records that this is what makes the engine run its schema commands inside a
  transaction.  The same module ships the two process-definition table
  descriptors, acm-process-definition-db-tables-1.0.xml and
  acm-process-definition-db-sql-1.0.xml.  The engine itself is built by
  activitiProcessEngine, from which the repository, runtime, task, history and
  management services are obtained.  Process definitions are deployed by
  activityClassPathBpmnDeployer, which resolves them through
  activitiPathMatchingResolver against the pattern held in
  activitiResourceExpression and registers them through
  processDefinitionManagementService and acmProcessDefinitionDao.  Two
  pre-parse handlers, acmActivitiProcessHandler and acmActivitiUserTaskHandler,
  attach the listener set during parsing, and two event bridges,
  acmActivitiEventListener and acmActivitiEntityEventListener, are registered on
  the configuration.  All of these names are behaviour-bearing and are preserved
  by the normalisation policy above.
- definitions-expected is 36, and that figure was re-derived here rather than
  adopted.  The authoritative measurement reads the corpus out of version
  control at the capture commit, because a git tree carries no build output:
  git ls-tree -r --name-only c8f6226105 | grep -c '\.bpmn'   ->  36
  A working-tree measurement with build and dependency directories pruned agrees:
  find . \( -name target -o -name node_modules -o -name .git \) -prune -o \( -name '*.bpmn*' -type f -print \) | wc -l   ->  36
- The plain working-tree form of that command returns a different figure in this
  tree, and the difference is recorded rather than smoothed over:
  find . -name '*.bpmn*' -not -path '*/node_modules/*' | wc -l   ->  72
  This tree has been built, so that form additionally counts the build copy of
  every definition beneath a module's output directory.  Both forms return 36 in
  a tree carrying no build output.  The divergence is a property of the working
  tree, not of the corpus, which is why the pruned and version-control forms are
  primary and the plain form is not.  The expected figure is 36, the verified
  figure is 36, and the corpus is unchanged by this migration.  The sibling
  notes/corpus-figures.txt records the same 36 from the same pruned form.
- The verified per-module breakdown of those 36 definitions, so a short load is
  attributable rather than merely visible: acm-foia 11; acm-privacy 8;
  acm-activiti-configuration 5, all in test resources; acm-service-ecm 3;
  acm-complaint-plugin 1, a default complaint workflow; acm-task-plugin 5, of
  which three are main resources - a buckslip process, a corrective action plan
  and a corrective task action plan - and two are test resources;
  acm-personnel-security-plugin 1; acm-service-media-engine 1;
  acm-service-transcribe 1.  The engine must load all 36.
- The governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken build
  exit zero."  This region is where that rule bites hardest.  A deployment can
  reach ready state, answer its readiness probe and return a healthy status
  while the engine has loaded fewer definitions than the corpus contains,
  because a short load does not necessarily stop the application from starting.
  The definitions-loaded count is the evidence.  The deployment's exit status is
  not, a healthy readiness probe is not, and an absence of error lines is not
  either.  A count below 36 is a finding even when everything else looks well.
- The timing trap, recorded explicitly because it is the single easiest way to
  produce a wrong count in this region.  The first startup takes 5 to 10
  minutes; the repository states this at README.md:141 and repeats it at
  docs/setup.md:108, both anchored at the capture commit.  The engine deploys
  its process definitions during that window.  Querying the engine or
  truncating this capture before the window has completed yields a
  definitions-loaded figure that is an artefact of timing rather than of
  behaviour, and it will usually look like a short load.  The capture must
  therefore span the whole window, and the sibling readiness-poll.log must show
  readiness reached before any flow was attempted.  A reviewer comparing the
  baseline and migrated counts needs to know that both captures waited, which is
  why the constraint is stated here and not left to the producer's discretion.
- Risk classification, and it is deliberately not softened.  The engine is the
  oldest load-bearing component in the whole reactor, dating from 2014, and its
  version is unchanged by this migration.  It could not be bootstrapped for
  testing without a database, so it was classified honestly as residual
  unverified risk and assigned to the deployment and smoke gates rather than
  being resolved during analysis.  Engine initialisation happens at deployment,
  before any request is made, which means this log region is the only place its
  behaviour is observed at all.  Because this region is SKIPPED, that
  observation has not been made, and the risk stands exactly where it was
  assigned.  Nothing in this file may be read as the engine having been shown to
  work on the new runtime.
- Three findings BOUND that concern.  They bound it; they do not remove it, and
  none of them is a substitute for the observation this region exists to make.
- Bounding finding 1, and its inference is WITHDRAWN as false rather than
  quietly dropped.  The two measurements it rests on are literally true and were
  re-derived over the corpus here: the corpus declares zero script tasks and
  zero script-format attributes, 0 and 0.  The inference originally drawn from
  those two zeros - that the engine therefore never requests a scripting engine,
  so the platform's removal of the bundled one cannot reach it - is FALSE.  The
  engine also reaches a scripting engine through a TASK LISTENER, and one main
  resource, personnelSecurityBackgroundInvestigation_v11.bpmn20.xml in
  acm-personnel-security-plugin, declares seven complete-event script task
  listeners, each carrying a language field whose value is javascript.  Measured
  at the capture commit: seven listener declarations and seven javascript
  language fields in that one file, and it is the only file in the corpus with
  any.  The reason a census misses them is worth recording, because it is the
  transferable part: the language is carried in a nested field element with
  CDATA content, not in an XML attribute, so a pattern-based search over
  attributes returns zero while the declarations sit there in plain sight.  A
  pattern-based census over configuration is only as good as its knowledge of
  every element that can reach the capability.  The bounding effect of the two
  zeros is therefore much narrower than it first appeared, and stating the
  withdrawal raises the residual risk rather than lowering it.
- Bounding finding 2.  The engine's persistence layer reflects over the
  application's own domain classes through ordinary, unrestricted reflection.
  It does not reflect across a module boundary into a package that is not
  exported, which is the pattern that fails under the new runtime's strong
  encapsulation.  This bounds the encapsulation exposure; it does not
  demonstrate that the engine initialises.
- Bounding finding 3.  The engine reads no class bytes and touches no
  JDK-internal packages.  It is therefore not exposed to the class file version
  change directly, and it is not exposed to the encapsulation of internal
  packages.  Again this bounds the concern; it says nothing about whether the
  process definitions deploy.
- The script-engine defect, which is registered and NOT fixed, and which is the
  most dangerous of the three platform removals precisely because it fails
  silently.  A scripting engine was removed from the platform in a later
  release.  Two application classes still request it by name: one in the FOIA
  standard application at line 94, and one in the privacy standard application
  at line 98, each inside a no-argument constructor immediately preceded by the
  instantiation of a script-engine manager.  At the capture commit those are the
  only two occurrences of the engine's name across all Java, XML, properties,
  shell and YAML files in the repository, which was verified rather than assumed.
- Both call sites compile cleanly at the new release, because the scripting API
  remained in the platform while only the engine implementation was removed.
  The lookup returns null at runtime and the next dereference throws.  No
  compiler, and no static audit for internal-package references, would surface
  it.  The contrast with the other two removals is the point: those fail loudly
  at compile time, one with a missing-package error and one with an
  unexported-package error, so they cannot be shipped by accident.  This one
  can.
- Both call sites are provably unreachable dead code, and the verified finding
  is stronger than a summary reading of the configuration would suggest.  In the
  FOIA standard application the XML comment region opens at line 131 and closes
  at line 141.  Both declarations therefore sit inside the comment: the
  non-JavaScript predicate at line 131 and the JavaScript-evaluating predicate
  at line 139 alike.  There is no active declaration of either one anywhere in
  that file.  The only consumer, a bean reference at line 150, sits inside a
  commented property block spanning lines 148 to 152.  The privacy standard
  application has no XML reference to the class whatsoever.  The references that
  appear to arrive from other modules are bean examples inside documentation
  comments rather than live configuration.
- At the capture commit no standalone script-engine dependency is declared, and
  the reasons that decision was taken are recorded because they are rule-driven
  rather than incidental.  Declaring one would introduce a library into the
  production dependency graph solely to support code that cannot execute, which
  the constraint against unjustified dependency changes forbids.  It would also
  make dead code functional, which is a behaviour CHANGE relative to the
  baseline and therefore forbidden by the baseline-as-tie-breaker rule.  And it
  would mask a latent defect instead of recording it, which the rule on
  discovered defects forbids.  The defect is registered rather than repaired.
- The consequence for this capture, stated on baseline reasoning rather than on
  the withdrawn inference.  At the capture commit the region must show no
  null-script-engine error, and the reason is simply that on the older runtime
  the bundled scripting engine is still part of the platform, so the lookup
  those two constructors perform cannot return null there.  The defect is latent
  at baseline and becomes reachable only once the removal applies.  It would be
  wrong to rest this expectation on bounding finding 1, whose inference is
  withdrawn above; the seven script task listeners mean the engine does have a
  path to a scripting engine, so the correct baseline statement is that the
  engine is present on this runtime, not that nothing asks for it.
- Every engine warning and error line is to be captured exactly as observed,
  with nothing repaired, nothing retried into submission and nothing hidden.
  This region carries the engine's own severity lines only.  The consolidated
  scan across every region is allocated to the sibling error-scan.txt, which
  reads from this same startup window; that allocation is where a reader goes
  for all severity lines at once, and naming it here is a statement of
  territory rather than a claim that the file has been produced on this run.
  Because this region is SKIPPED there are no engine severity lines to carry.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers engine initialisation and
  process-definition deployment only.  Container and application startup belong
  to catalina.out.log, context initialisation to spring-context.log, annotation
  scanning to reflection-scan.log, persistence initialisation to jpa-init.log
  and the startup asset build to frontend-build.log.  Toolchain provenance
  belongs to env/toolchain.txt, artifact digests to artifacts/ and archived
  unit-test reports to surefire/.  The flow-7 request and response capture is a
  separate matter again and lives in flow-7-workflow-start.out,
  flow-7-workflow-start.status and flow-7-workflow-start.result.txt; this file
  records engine initialisation, which happens before any request is made, and
  none of that flow's content is reproduced here.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and git matches those at any depth: logs, bin, target,
  activemq-data and partition-descriptor are all ignored wherever they appear,
  as is builds/.  The ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the
  .log file extension, so a .log file placed inside a directory named logs would
  be silently uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This file
  therefore sits directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath
  it.  The trap is sharpest in this region of all: activemq-data and
  partition-descriptor are both engine-adjacent names, and grouping engine
  artefacts under either of them would look tidy and lose the evidence.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule against relying on
  JDK-internal access is, at commit c8f6226105, the block at README.md:111-127,
  duplicated at docs/setup.md:76-96.  It is described here and cited by anchor,
  and deliberately never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries
  credential-shaped values, and it also carries two pre-existing unbalanced
  quotation marks, at README.md:119 and spanning README.md:123-124.  Importing
  that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no benefit,
  and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is neither
  repaired nor imported.
- Verified at the capture commit: that launch configuration contains zero
  module-access JVM arguments.  If such an argument ever appeared in a captured
  banner it would be replaced, in this region, by the description "a
  module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK
  package to the unnamed module", and the substitution would be recorded in this
  notes block.  No such substitution was required here, because nothing was
  captured and because the audited configuration carries no such argument.  The
  register of applied exceptions is referred to only by its title, which is
  JDK Access Exceptions, and never by filename or path, so that this file
  introduces no occurrence of the very argument names the register accounts for.
- The governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs
  discovered during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks
  a validation item."  Applied to this region that means the script-engine
  defect above is registered and left alone, the withdrawn inference is recorded
  rather than deleted, and every engine warning is captured as observed.  The
  migration's escape clause is invoked exactly twice in the entire change set
  and neither invocation is in this folder.  Nothing in this file proposes,
  implies or performs a fix; in particular nothing here suggests declaring a
  script engine or repairing either of the two call sites.
- The governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed
  behavior at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and
  each resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase
  this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution changes no
  meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is the whole reason this file exists before any migrated counterpart
  does, and the reason an absence is recorded here instead of a plausible-looking
  substitute: an uninitialised engine's behaviour is unknowable rather than
  assumable.  It is also the rule that forced the withdrawal recorded above, in
  the direction that increases the recorded risk rather than the direction that
  would have been more comfortable.
- The governing rule on justified change applies to this region in an unusual
  way, and it is why the region matters at all.  A dependency change without a
  specific compatibility reason is out of scope, so the engine's version is
  deliberately held rather than advanced.  Holding it is a decision that needs
  evidence, and this capture is the only runtime observation that would supply
  it.  A SKIPPED region therefore leaves that decision resting on static
  analysis alone, which is precisely why the risk is recorded as assigned to
  this gate rather than as resolved.  The same rule keeps the capture pipeline to
  a POSIX toolset: no JSON processor, no scripting runtime, no test framework
  and no package installation are used to produce this region.
- The governing rule against silently skipping anything is what this file's
  shape implements.  Nothing has been quietly omitted: the region that could not
  be captured says so in a machine-readable field, names the specific unmet
  preconditions, and distinguishes them from the preconditions that were met so
  that the reason cannot be misread.
- Namespace invariance holds in this file as everywhere else in the change set:
  no reference to the forbidden target namespace appears anywhere here,
  including inside the capture delimiters, which are empty.
- Provenance of the rules quoted above, and both facts belong together because
  either alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility reports, verbatim,
  "No user rules provided" - there is no on-disk user rules document for this
  project, and that is explicitly not licence to lower the bar.  Second, rules
  are nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed an explicit,
  numbered block of seven rules that governs this work in full exactly as an
  external rules document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  The
  identifiers the migration plan uses for them are its own navigational
  convention rather than quoted titles, which is why the rules are quoted here
  by their text rather than cited by number alone.
